                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "**Broken Sword 2: The Smoking Mirror** is what happens when you survive a global conspiracy in the first game and decide, \"You know what? I'm not done being confused by ancient history.\"\r\n\r\nGeorge Hazard returns, this time trading the chic streets of Paris for the jungles of Mexico, proving that George is essentially a magnet for archaeological disasters. If there is a hidden city or a cursed Aztec mirror in a five-thousand-mile radius, George will find it, trip over it, and then accidentally offend the local population while trying to be helpful.\r\n\r\nThe gameplay follows the classic \"adventure game logic\" where the solution to a puzzle is never the logical one, but always the one that makes you say, \"Why on earth would that work?\" You spend your time collecting items that have no business being together—like a piece of string and a very specific type of fruit—and hoping that combining them will somehow stop an ancient deity from waking up and deleting the world.\r\n\r\nThe banter between George and Nico is still the heart of the game, providing a lovely romantic tension that is frequently interrupted by the fact that they are currently being hunted by people who really don't like them.\r\n\r\nIt’s a wonderful, atmospheric journey that captures the feeling of an Indiana Jones movie if Indy had a slightly more neurotic personality and a penchant for talking to statues. **8/10. I have no idea how the Aztec mythology works now, but I’m just glad I survived the trip without getting eaten by a jaguar.**",
